1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,787
We want Copilot to find why a zero
quantity passes.

2
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:08,723
We start with a plain question and no
explicit reference to the issue or the

3
00:00:08,723 --> 00:00:09,662
relevant files.

4
00:00:09,842 --> 00:00:14,566
Depending on the session, Copilot may have
access to some editor context.

5
00:00:14,746 --> 00:00:18,873
We can make the evidence easier to inspect
by choosing it ourselves.

6
00:00:19,053 --> 00:00:21,553
Watch which sources we add to the request.

7
00:00:23,007 --> 00:00:25,092
Our issue tells us what should happen.

8
00:00:25,272 --> 00:00:27,464
The validator shows what does happen.

9
00:00:27,644 --> 00:00:31,061
The test file shows which inputs the suite
already covers.

10
00:00:31,241 --> 00:00:33,921
Those three sources are enough for this
task.

11
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:37,920
We leave unrelated files, keys, and logs
out of the chat.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:42,894
In your organization, confirm that the
sources you attach are approved for this

13
00:00:42,894 --> 00:00:43,986
model and service.

14
00:00:45,465 --> 00:00:50,638
In Copilot Chat, use the context picker or
select the named files directly.

15
00:00:50,818 --> 00:00:55,135
Attach the short issue text or link
through a method available in your

16
00:00:55,135 --> 00:00:56,430
approved environment.

17
00:00:56,610 --> 00:01:00,546
Before submitting, verify the file names
displayed in the chat.

18
00:01:00,726 --> 00:01:06,094
Ask Copilot to separate what the code does
from what the issue requires and to point

19
00:01:06,094 --> 00:01:07,819
to the relevant comparison.

20
00:01:09,298 --> 00:01:12,661
The answer points to the greater than or
equal comparison.

21
00:01:12,841 --> 00:01:16,915
Open the file and check that it really
contains that comparison.

22
00:01:17,095 --> 00:01:20,627
Then read the test file and confirm that
zero is missing.

23
00:01:20,807 --> 00:01:25,542
Copilot can identify a likely story that
still needs verification.

24
00:01:25,722 --> 00:01:30,087
File references help you trace the
reasoning, and the source remains the

25
00:01:30,087 --> 00:01:32,208
authority for the current behavior.

26
00:01:33,673 --> 00:01:38,726
If an answer mentions a different version
of the validator, check the branch and the

27
00:01:38,726 --> 00:01:40,531
attached file before retrying.

28
00:01:40,711 --> 00:01:44,688
Start a fresh chat when an old discussion
keeps steering the response.

29
00:01:44,868 --> 00:01:49,714
In the new session, attach the current
exact code and say which version of the

30
00:01:49,714 --> 00:01:50,583
issue applies.

31
00:01:50,763 --> 00:01:54,234
Keep the scope small to make the response
easier to verify.

32
00:01:55,715 --> 00:02:01,453
Suppose Copilot identifies why zero passes
but cannot verify the business rule.

33
00:02:01,633 --> 00:02:06,241
Ask which source it still needs and
provide the actual issue text through an

34
00:02:06,241 --> 00:02:07,212
approved method.

35
00:02:07,392 --> 00:02:10,838
We then ask it to show where the code and
rule disagree.

36
00:02:11,018 --> 00:02:15,746
The useful result is a traceable
explanation supported by both sources,

37
00:02:15,746 --> 00:02:18,742
with any remaining assumptions clearly
named.

38
00:02:20,215 --> 00:02:23,322
Ask the same training question in two
fresh chats.

39
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:25,701
In the first, give the issue only.

40
00:02:25,881 --> 00:02:29,315
In the second, include the current
validator and its tests.

41
00:02:29,495 --> 00:02:34,509
Then compare which answer locates the
actual cause and which admits uncertainty.

42
00:02:34,689 --> 00:02:38,754
Keep one piece of evidence from the code
and one from the test file.

43
00:02:38,934 --> 00:02:42,890
That record helps you decide what context
to supply next time.
